  "content": {
    "id": "p148-q17-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Show that the equation $x^2+(4a+1)x+2a=0$ will have two real solutions for any real values of $a$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Two distinct real solutions for every $a\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe discriminant is $\\Delta=16a^2+1\\ge1>0$.",
      "worked": "The coefficient of $x^2$ is $1$, so the equation is quadratic for every real $a$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(4a+1)^2-4(1)(2a)\\\\\\Delta&=16a^2+8a+1-8a\\\\\\Delta&=16a^2+1\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $a^2\\ge0$, $\\Delta\\ge1>0$ for every real $a$.\n\nHence, the equation has two distinct real solutions for every real $a$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 44
  },
